Somali refugee Bader Hassan arrived in  Yemen 30 years ago it's been his home  ever since he survived through five  years of war but the economic impact of  the corona virus pandemic has pushed his  already precarious existence to the  limit he says he has no choice but to  leave I've worked in a coffee shop I've  done lots of jobs but now I'm back to  washing cars but even that's limited  because of the corona virus wherever I  go they ask what's your nationality  yemeni somali i say somali and they say  sorry goodbye life has become really  difficult there are more than 280,000  refugees living in yemen many like  baddha come from somalia the UN says  refugees are facing increasingly  difficult living conditions in the past  the refugees were well welcomed by the  Yemeni communities and the authorities  of course with the dire level of poverty  and know the corona virus there is more  and more discrimination and scapegoating  of this population coming from East  Africa alongside the refugees around a  hundred thousand migrants arrived in  Yemen each year but that figure has  declined in recent months because of the  global pandemic many are pushed by war  and poverty in the Horn of Africa they  choose Yemen because of its close  location hoping to cross into wealthier  Gulf countries in search of a better  life but now that dream is over about  250 of us took a boat to Yemen that cost  a lot of money five people died on the  journey once we arrived we headed  towards Saudi Arabia but the road was  blocked by the Hootie's they made us  turn back we have nothing to eat and  drink people are tired of helping us  they left their home for what they hoped  would be a life-changing opportunity  abroad but many say they no longer feel  welcome in Yemen and their only option  is to leave  Victoria gate and be al-jazeera
 

